/// `secret-never-open` keeps a secret in the process that reads it: it never crosses a world, prints or compares.
///
/// Implemented: `std.secret` declares `Secret` with no mark, so a migration's result or capture holding a secret is refused `Immobile`, a run answering one prints opaque at its seal, and comparing two is refused `ModelUnanswered`, naming the secret's type; a site names its key's secret, and `gandr host --site` reads it through the `Secrets` model `--secrets` names, or from `GANDR_SECRET_<NAME>`.
/// Not implemented: a theory requiring `Secrets`, and a host interface carrying a secret; #3523 owes the first and #2901 the second.
///
/// The model below is a user's handler, answering each name with one sealed text.
/// A run of it answers opaque at the seal: only a host reads the text.
///
/// - see: corpus/pathological/worlds/secret-crosses.gandr
/// - see: corpus/model/worlds/two-processes.secrets.gandr
/// - see: crates/surface-driver/tests/net.rs

import "../../../library/std/src/secret.gandr";

/// A handler holding one secret for every name.
pub module Held : Model(Secrets) with sort K = Secret.T {
  /// The secret, sealed.
  def secret(name : String) -> Secret.T { Secret.seal("held") }
}

/// The secret `far` names: its run answers opaque at the seal.
@[ runs("⟨opaque at Secret⟩ : () => Secret.T") ]
def far() -> Secret.T { Held.secret("far") }
